meta {
  name: 05-notifications-journey
  seq: 6
}

docs {
  The notification lifecycle as a client experiences it: a badge count, a list,
  marking one read, marking the rest read, and the preference screen.

  **Why this belongs in the HTTP suite and not only in pytest.** Three of the
  properties here have no in-process equivalent:

  - **Route ordering.** `POST /notifications/read-all` and
    `POST /notifications/{id}/read` are both POSTs one segment deep. The literal
    route only wins because it is declared first; swap the two declarations and
    "read-all" is parsed as a notification id and answers 422. Only a real
    router resolving a real path can catch that regression.
  - **Delivery is asynchronous.** A notification is published through the outbox
    and delivered by a worker, so it appears some time after the request that
    caused it returned. In-process tests drain the outbox synchronously; a
    client polls. This folder polls, which is what a client actually does.
  - **Ownership is invisible in the URL.** Every query is scoped to the bearer
    token, so there is no id a client could supply to reach another user's
    notifications. 03-authorization proves the crossing is refused; here we
    prove the scoping itself holds on a live server.

  The pytest suite owns the rule matrix — every preference combination, every
  delivery decision. This folder owns the sequence.
}
